---
import EnglishBreadcrumb from '../../../../components/english/EnglishBreadcrumb.astro'
import { LakehouseTableStateLab } from '../../../../components/english/LakehouseTableStateLab'
import { buildEnglishLakehouseFixture } from '../../../../features/english-lakehouse/fixture'
import EnglishArticleLayout from '../../../../layouts/EnglishArticleLayout.astro'
import lakehouseStylesUrl from '../../../../styles/layouts/english-lakehouse.css?url'
import { getEnglishRoute } from '../../../../utils/english-routes'

const title = 'What Is a Lakehouse? Files, Tables & Engines | sql.sb'
const description =
  'A lakehouse is an architecture pattern, not a standard. See how data files, table metadata, catalogs and query engines fit in a bounded table-commit example.'
const fixture = buildEnglishLakehouseFixture()
---

<EnglishArticleLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  active="warehouse"
  styleUrls={[lakehouseStylesUrl]}
>
  <EnglishBreadcrumb
    items={[
      { label: 'English home', key: 'overview' },
      { label: 'Data warehouse', key: 'warehouse' },
      { label: 'Lakehouse' },
    ]}
  />

  <header class="english-article__header">
    <p class="english-article__eyebrow">Data warehouse · lakehouse pattern</p>
    <h1 class="english-article__title">
      What is a lakehouse? Files, table state, and query engines
    </h1>
    <p class="english-article__lede">
      A lakehouse is a useful name for an analytical architecture pattern: data files remain in
      lake-style storage while a table-management layer makes schema and committed table state
      explicit for compatible query engines. The term is not a universal architecture standard, and
      the exact boundaries depend on the implementation.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="english-answer">
    <span class="english-answer__label">Quick answer</span>
    <p>
      A lakehouse pattern combines flexible file storage with managed table semantics, such as a
      schema and a versioned set of files, so supported engines can query a defined table state. The
      files store data; table metadata describes which files belong to a table version; a catalog
      can help engines find the table; and a query engine executes the read. These are related but
      distinct responsibilities—not one interchangeable product or a promise of universal
      transactions or engine compatibility.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="english-article__body">
    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="roles">
      <h2 id="roles">Four responsibilities sit between stored bytes and a query result</h2>
      <p>
        A file can be readable without telling every reader which schema or file set represents the
        current table. A table format and its metadata add a table-state contract; a catalog and a
        query engine may participate too, but they do different jobs.
      </p>
      <div class="english-table-scroll">
        <table class="english-table">
          <caption>Separate the data, table, discovery, and execution roles</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Responsibility</th>
              <th scope="col">What it contributes</th>
              <th scope="col">What it does not do by itself</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Data files</th>
              <td>
                Store records as bytes in a file format such as Parquet, JSON, or CSV. The teaching
                fixture below models a small mobile-banking event table.
              </td>
              <td>A directory of files alone does not define the current table version.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Table format and metadata</th>
              <td>
                Describe table schema and, in supported implementations, the data files and
                snapshots that make up a table state.
              </td>
              <td>It does not execute a SQL query or provide compute on its own.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Catalog</th>
              <td>
                Help identify or discover a table and locate its metadata through an
                implementation's catalog interface.
              </td>
              <td>Its exact role and boundary vary; a catalog is not the query engine.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Query engine</th>
              <td>
                Plans and executes a query, reading table metadata and data files through a
                compatible integration.
              </td>
              <td>
                Reading one format does not imply support for every format feature or commit path.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        In a particular deployment, some responsibilities may be packaged together or exposed
        through different interfaces. Keep the concepts separate when tracing who stores bytes,
        defines table state, resolves a table name, and runs the query.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="table-state">
      <h2 id="table-state">Follow one table update from files to reader visibility</h2>
      <p>
        This fixed example reuses the existing <code>mobile_banking_events</code> teaching fixture.
        Version 1 has two sample event rows and four fields. An update adds a <code>channel</code>{' '}
        field and a new sample event. The separate file batch models ten files, with the sixth as a
        failure point; the file count is not a row count or a one-file-per-row mapping.
      </p>
      <LakehouseTableStateLab client:load fixture={fixture} />
      <p>
        The failed case leaves five files written before the error, but they are not included in a
        committed v2 snapshot in this model. Their later cleanup is not assumed. On a successful
        table-level commit, the example publishes v2; querying historical v1 changes this read's
        target, not the current published pointer.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="scope">
      <h2 id="scope">A table commit has a boundary</h2>
      <p>
        A supported integration can define how a particular table update becomes a committed
        version. That is a table-level statement about the format, storage, catalog, and engine path
        involved—not a guarantee of one distributed transaction across ingestion, transformations,
        multiple tables, or unrelated services. Implementations differ in their commit protocols,
        concurrency handling, and supported operations.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Snapshot history can make earlier table states addressable, but retention policy still
          determines which history remains available.
        </li>
        <li>
          A schema change can be represented as a table-state change; engine behavior for that
          change depends on compatible implementations and the features in use.
        </li>
        <li>
          Open or interoperable formats do not mean every engine can read and write every feature
          with equal support. Verify the actual engine, catalog, format, and version combination.
        </li>
        <li>
          Table metadata does not automatically provide governance, permissions, data quality,
          lineage, file cleanup, or a performance improvement.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="english-callout" data-tone="info">
        <strong>
          The example is a table-state model, not a cross-system transaction guarantee
        </strong>
        <p>
          It reuses the repository's deterministic teaching fixture to show one supported commit
          boundary. It does not measure a storage engine, estimate runtime, or claim that all
          lakehouse implementations behave identically.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="examples">
      <h2 id="examples">Treat implementation names as examples, not synonyms</h2>
      <p>
        {fixture.formatExamples.join(', ')} are examples of table-management projects used in
        lakehouse discussions. They have distinct specifications, protocols, and integration
        behavior; this page does not compare them or promise feature parity. For implementation
        details, check the relevant project's current documentation:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="https://iceberg.apache.org/spec/">Apache Iceberg format specification</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://github.com/delta-io/delta/blob/master/PROTOCOL.md">
            Delta Lake transaction protocol
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://hudi.apache.org/docs/timeline/">Apache Hudi timeline documentation</a>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Specifications and compatibility evolve. A broad description of a table-management pattern
        is not a substitute for checking a concrete deployment's supported operations and
        integration boundaries.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-related" aria-labelledby="related">
      <h2 id="related">Related pages</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('warehouseLayers')}>Data warehouse layers</a>
          <p>How staging, core, and mart responsibilities differ from a storage/table pattern.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('etlVsElt')}>ETL vs ELT</a>
          <p>Where transformation happens relative to the warehouse load boundary.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('idempotentEtl')}>Idempotent ETL</a>
          <p>Why write repeatability and recovery are separate from a table format.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
</EnglishArticleLayout>
